.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 
Represents a user-created \fRthread pool\fP\&. 

You can execute functions explicitly within this \fBThreadPool\fP using \fBThreadPool::submit\fP\&. \fBThreadPool::submit\fP executes a closure in one of the \fBThreadPool's\fP threads\&.

.PP
When this \fBThreadPool\fP is destructed, that's a signal for the threads it manages to terminate, they will complete executing any remaining work that you have spawned, and automatically terminate\&.

.PP
\fBThread safety\fP
.RS 4
\fBThreadPool::submit\fP, \fBThreadPool::try_submit\fP, \fBThreadPool::shutdown()\fP, and \fBThreadPool::is_shutting_down\fP may be called concurrently while this object's lifetime remains active\&.
.RE
.PP
Blocking shutdown using \fBThreadPool::shutdown(bool)\fP with \fRwait == true\fP must not execute concurrently with another blocking shutdown or with destruction\&.

.PP
Destruction must not race with any operation on this object\&. Blocking shutdown and destruction must not be initiated from a worker thread owned by this same pool\&.

.PP
\fBTask execution\fP
.RS 4
Tasks may submit additional tasks to this pool\&. However, tasks should not synchronously wait for futures produced by this same pool, as this can exhaust all workers and deadlock\&.
.RE
.PP
Submitted functions and arguments are decay-copied into task storage\&. References must be passed explicitly with \fBstd::ref\fP and must remain valid until task execution completes\&.

.PP
Shutdown is cooperative\&. It does not forcibly interrupt executing user code, so shutdown may block indefinitely if a task never returns\&. 
.PP
